course: dictionary
lesson: 130
title: Adverbs: words that tell us more
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An adverb is a word that tells us more about a verb. It can tell us how, when, where or how often something happens. «She ran quickly to the bus stop.» The word quickly tells us how she ran, so it is an adverb.
explain: Adverbs can also describe an adjective or another adverb. In «very tall», very tells us how tall. In «The sun shone very brightly», very tells us how brightly. Ask yourself: what does this word tell me about the action or the description?
explain: Many adverbs end in -ly, but not all of them do. Fast, soon and often are adverbs too. Some -ly words are not adverbs at all: in «a friendly dog», friendly describes the noun dog, so it is an adjective. Always check what the word is describing.
fact: An adverb tells us more about a verb: how, when, where or how often.
fact: Adverbs can describe adjectives or other adverbs, as in «very tall» and «really slowly».
fact: «Quickly» tells us how, «yesterday» tells us when, «here» tells us where, and «often» tells us how often.
fact: Many adverbs end in -ly, but «fast», «soon» and «often» are adverbs that do not.
fact: A strong verb can sometimes replace a weak verb plus an adverb: «walked slowly» can become «dawdled».
exercise: «She ran quickly to the bus stop.» Which word is the adverb? | stop | *quickly | she | bus
exercise: «The sun shone very brightly.» What does very tell us? | *It tells us more about the adverb brightly | It is a verb showing an action | It names a place | It tells us more about the noun sun
exercise: «The bus will arrive tomorrow.» Which adverb tells us when? | *tomorrow | bus | arrive
test: Which single verb can replace «walked slowly»? | dawdlingly | *dawdled | slowly | walked quickly
test: In «a friendly dog», is friendly an adverb? | Yes, because it tells how | Yes, because it ends in -ly | *No, it describes the noun dog | No, because it is a verb
exam: «The dog barked loudly outside.» Which adverb tells us how the dog barked? | outside | barked | *loudly | dog
